[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one bug, one fix queued. Its reports hold three
problems. The queued parser fix closes the first. The other two stay open after it ships.

<Callout kind="warn">
Do not close #4471's reports when the parser fix ships. Close each report against the ticket of
its own problem below.
</Callout>

## The problems in #4471

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

Counts are built from `reports-4471.json` at render time, one report per id: the export pages
overlap, so some reports come twice. A report belongs to a problem by **its tenant and how it came
in** (`causes.ts`, `split.ts`), not by intake's tag. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser,
and a browser set to `en-US` says nothing about the tenant's date order.

## 1 · The parser reads day-first dates month first

Every tenant in #4471 writes the day first. The shared parser reads month first, so 7 April
becomes 4 July. Any date with a day of 12 or less flips; a day above 12 cannot be a month, so
those dates survive. This is the fix already queued.

## 2 · booking-v3 tells day-first tenants to type month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. On a day-first tenant (`alder.physio`,
`copper.studio`, `pale.harbour`) people type what the hint says, and the form reads it the
tenant's way. The result looks the same as problem 1: day and month swapped. **The parser fix does
not touch it.** The fix is the hint, in the form: print the tenant's own order. booking-v2 prints
`DD/MM/YYYY` and is not affected. Only reports sent **through the booking-v3 form** belong here;
the same tenants' app and email reports are problem 1.

## 3 · Dates move by one day

These reports are not a swap. The invite and the calendar disagree by exactly one day: 12 May
shows as 11 May, 6 October as Monday 5 October, one attendee in Lisbon gets Tuesday and one in
Toronto gets Monday, an invite arrives before the booking was made, every booking in June is a
day off. That is a time zone or daylight-saving shift, not a date order. The desk noted these
reports itself (`desk-notes.json`, `oneDayMove`), then filed them under problem 1 by tenant.
Neither fix moves them back.

## How we got one ticket

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line, and every one of
these reports says some version of "wrong date". Same subject is not same cause. The
[triage explorer](/kb/triage/dedupe) shows it: same subject, different tenant gives different
causes.

## Lessons

- A ticket count is not a bug count. Split by cause before you mark a big ticket known.
- Read the desk's own notes: the third problem was already written down.

